# Cargo.lock is committed on purpose: this workspace ships a binary, and the VHS
# reference images (V001) are only comparable if the build resolves identically.
/target

# Scratch output from manually running a tape at the top level of tapes/ (e.g.
# `vhs tapes/_dimensions.tape`) — GIF/PNG side effects of a probe or calibration
# run, not the committed references. Real per-screen references (V005) live
# under tapes/artifacts/ and are unaffected by this non-recursive pattern.
/tapes/*.gif
/tapes/*.png

# Agent worktrees. `EnterWorktree` puts them here, and a git worktree carries
# its own `.git` file — so `git status` showed `?? .claude/worktrees/` as one
# opaque untracked directory rather than recursing into it. Two consequences,
# and the second is why this line exists: `git add -A` would have staged an
# entire second checkout, and `scripts/lint-repo-hygiene.sh`'s untracked-file
# checks walk `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`, where only the
# `[ -f "$file" ]` guard (directories are skipped) kept it from scanning six
# copies of `target/`. `/target` is root-anchored and does not cover
# `.claude/worktrees/*/target`. Ignoring them outright is the honest state:
# they are scratch checkouts and their commits live on master or nowhere.
/.claude/worktrees/

# CPython bytecode. `S4` put a python fixture in a tests/ directory for the
# first time — `crates/phosphor/tests/fixtures/toy_language_server.py`, the
# language server the pty tests drive — and running it leaves a `__pycache__`
# beside it. Untracked, invisible to `just gate`, and a blanket `git add -A`
# would have committed a 7 KB `.pyc` built for whichever interpreter happened
# to run. `scripts/lint-repo-hygiene.sh` would not have caught it: it bans
# tracked files over 1 MB, and this is 7 KB of the wrong thing rather than too
# much of the right one.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
